WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.062 --> 00:00:03.240
[whistle blowing]

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:05.240
What she's managing now is superhuman.

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:07.173
She would have lost her manual dexterity.

00:00:07.173 --> 00:00:08.465
Hi, my name's John Hudson.

00:00:08.465 --> 00:00:10.873
I'm the UK Military's
chief survival instructor,

00:00:10.873 --> 00:00:13.311
land or sea, Arctic, desert or jungle.

00:00:13.311 --> 00:00:15.162
And I train the trainers how to teach that

00:00:15.162 --> 00:00:16.680
to everyone else in the UK Military.

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:17.538
Today, we're going to be looking at

00:00:17.538 --> 00:00:19.229
ocean-survival scenes in movies,

00:00:19.229 --> 00:00:21.078
and we're going to be
judging how real they are.

00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:27.620
That looks to me like
they're diving vertically

00:00:27.620 --> 00:00:29.052
at the sea, and nobody will survive.

00:00:29.052 --> 00:00:30.440
It would be like hitting concrete.

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:32.807
What tends to happen in airplane ditchings

00:00:32.807 --> 00:00:34.790
is you're just trying to do a
controlled landing on water,

00:00:34.790 --> 00:00:37.697
like Sully did, like Tom
Hanks does, on the Hudson.

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:41.930
The water's gonna flush backwards.

00:00:41.930 --> 00:00:44.271
The best place for Tom
Hanks, or any of us, to sit

00:00:44.271 --> 00:00:45.429
in that sort of scenario

00:00:45.429 --> 00:00:46.992
is probably near an over-wing exit.

00:00:46.992 --> 00:00:49.290
Definitely be near an exit.

00:00:49.290 --> 00:00:50.739
If you take cold water,

00:00:50.739 --> 00:00:52.893
you can only hold your breath
for about five or 10 seconds.

00:00:52.893 --> 00:00:55.940
And the lethal dose for
salt water is 1 1/2 liters.

00:00:55.940 --> 00:00:57.710
So if you're in an airplane
and you're not near the exit,

00:00:57.710 --> 00:00:59.310
and it fills with --
or a helicopter --

00:00:59.310 --> 00:01:02.026
and it fills with cold
water, and you go [gasps],

00:01:02.026 --> 00:01:03.721
you're going to die, because you've taken

00:01:03.721 --> 00:01:06.153
the lethal dose of salt
water into your lungs.

00:01:06.153 --> 00:01:07.224
Have a feel.

00:01:07.224 --> 00:01:08.783
You check next time you're on an airplane.

00:01:08.783 --> 00:01:11.260
When they say, "Your life
preserver is under your seat,"

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:13.385
have a look around and
see if anyone checks.

00:01:13.385 --> 00:01:14.218
I always do.

00:01:14.218 --> 00:01:15.347
Remember, in a sinking airplane, though,

00:01:15.347 --> 00:01:17.354
do not inflate it until you're outside.

00:01:17.354 --> 00:01:19.963
Because as it sinks, you'll be
trapped inside the fuselage.

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:26.750
Another bit of Hollywoodism,

00:01:26.750 --> 00:01:29.800
because all of these life
rafts have a weak link on them.

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:32.880
So if there's any strain like
this put on the life raft,

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:35.470
the cord will tear away
without ripping the life raft,

00:01:35.470 --> 00:01:37.160
so that if it's attached
to a sinking airplane

00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:39.157
and the airplane sinks, you
don't get dragged down with it.

00:01:39.157 --> 00:01:40.882
It's built in. It's a
clever design feature.

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:48.054
He can hold his breath
because he's in the tropics.

00:01:48.054 --> 00:01:50.200
But it's at night and it's in
a storm, so in all likelihood,

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:51.183
the air temperature and everything else,

00:01:51.183 --> 00:01:52.016
gonna be a bit cold.

00:01:52.016 --> 00:01:53.160
It will churn the sea up a little bit.

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:54.385
You'll get that mixing.

00:01:54.385 --> 00:01:56.392
So it won't be warm water that he's in.

00:01:56.392 --> 00:01:59.440
It still is gonna limit how
long he can hold his breath for.

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:01.709
It's a little bit of Hollywood
going on, I think, this bit.

00:02:01.709 --> 00:02:04.626
[turbine spinning]

00:02:06.550 --> 00:02:07.813
In that kind of storm,

00:02:07.813 --> 00:02:10.212
is the fuel likely to have caught fire?

00:02:10.212 --> 00:02:11.353
Probably not.

00:02:11.353 --> 00:02:12.658
But it makes for a good scene, doesn't it?

00:02:12.658 --> 00:02:13.858
It looks cool.

00:02:13.858 --> 00:02:16.140
The airplane's landed with such force

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:18.253
that it smashed apart and caught fire.

00:02:18.253 --> 00:02:19.610
And yet, we've still got an engine,

00:02:19.610 --> 00:02:22.880
which weighs several
tons, above the water.

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:25.340
So, we're kind of playing
with time and reality

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:26.750
for a brilliant scene here.

00:02:26.750 --> 00:02:28.825
It's only gonna get 4
out of 10 for realism.

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:33.239
Is there anyone alive out there?

00:02:33.239 --> 00:02:35.063
This is people from the Carpathia

00:02:35.063 --> 00:02:37.280
coming to try and find
survivors from the Titanic.

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:39.210
What signs of life
you'll be able to detect,

00:02:39.210 --> 00:02:41.390
maybe a ripple or some small movement.

00:02:41.390 --> 00:02:43.390
But by this point, people
are going to be unconscious.

00:02:43.390 --> 00:02:45.480
There's no way you can survive
in water that temperature

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:46.848
for even more than an hour.

00:02:46.848 --> 00:02:49.040
You know, half of us will
be dead within 45 minutes.

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:50.357
So the only people who would have survived

00:02:50.357 --> 00:02:52.663
were those who were
floating on or in something,

00:02:52.663 --> 00:02:54.098
rather than the water itself.

00:02:54.950 --> 00:02:56.416
[whimpering]

00:02:56.416 --> 00:02:58.207
You can see the ice in her
hair. That's realistic.

00:02:58.207 --> 00:03:01.270
When we do ice-breaking drills
in the Arctic with our guys,

00:03:01.270 --> 00:03:02.738
soon as you get out of the frozen lake,

00:03:02.738 --> 00:03:04.892
your hair is like glass straightaway.

00:03:04.892 --> 00:03:07.410
But, if you both wanted to survive,

00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:09.459
your best chance is to
both try and get on.

00:03:09.459 --> 00:03:10.584
It'll be a little bit wobbly,

00:03:10.584 --> 00:03:11.600
but you're gonna lie still.

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:13.948
And we know the sea was
super calm that night,

00:03:13.948 --> 00:03:15.869
so you've got a good
chance of getting on there.

00:03:15.869 --> 00:03:18.136
If you can get the big
arteries away from the water,

00:03:18.136 --> 00:03:20.570
like your femoral arteries
or under your armpits

00:03:20.570 --> 00:03:22.594
and round your neck,
you'll survive for longer.

00:03:22.594 --> 00:03:23.866
Come back.

00:03:24.799 --> 00:03:26.840
Come back! Come back!

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:28.641
What could she do to raise his attention

00:03:28.641 --> 00:03:30.358
while she's actually on there?

00:03:30.358 --> 00:03:33.428
Maybe she's got something
she can clank together

00:03:33.428 --> 00:03:35.978
to raise a metallic noise
that would carry a long way.

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:42.606
That point there, where
she jumps into the water,

00:03:42.606 --> 00:03:44.986
what happens to humans on
immersion in cold water

00:03:44.986 --> 00:03:48.760
is that you have an absolutely
uncontrolled gasp response.

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:49.900
Absolutely uncontrollable.

00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:51.730
So, the main problem with Hollywood films

00:03:51.730 --> 00:03:54.010
about cold-water survival
is that they never gasp

00:03:54.010 --> 00:03:56.599
and they never hyperventilate
for about a minute afterwards.

00:03:56.599 --> 00:03:58.533
And it's absolutely uncontrollable.

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:03.850
She probably wouldn't
have been able to swim

00:04:03.850 --> 00:04:04.991
initially, at first.

00:04:04.991 --> 00:04:06.462
We've tested Olympic athletes

00:04:06.462 --> 00:04:07.570
at the Institute of Naval Medicine

00:04:07.570 --> 00:04:09.118
on how people respond to this,

00:04:09.118 --> 00:04:11.583
and gold and silver
medalists can't do this.

00:04:11.583 --> 00:04:13.848
[man yelling]

00:04:13.848 --> 00:04:15.851
[whistle blowing]

00:04:15.851 --> 00:04:17.921
What she's managing now is superhuman.

00:04:17.921 --> 00:04:20.041
She would have lost her manual dexterity.

00:04:20.041 --> 00:04:22.210
Absolutely no chance of
being able to do that

00:04:22.210 --> 00:04:23.150
when you're that cold.

00:04:23.150 --> 00:04:24.918
This is the Inuit sign for greeting,

00:04:24.918 --> 00:04:26.588
proving that you've got manual dexterity.

00:04:26.588 --> 00:04:29.460
Which is why, grimly, in
the bottom of a life raft,

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:31.202
you often find human teeth,

00:04:31.202 --> 00:04:32.733
because it's impossible to open packets.

00:04:32.733 --> 00:04:35.647
People try to rip them
out with fists and teeth.

00:04:35.647 --> 00:04:39.052
So, to adjust a small
whistle into your lips,

00:04:39.052 --> 00:04:41.534
when it's gonna be frozen
to that guy's lips anyway,

00:04:41.534 --> 00:04:44.443
and then stick it in your
own mouth, super unlikely.

00:04:44.443 --> 00:04:45.693
That's why in modern life rafts

00:04:45.693 --> 00:04:46.893
we're given plastic whistles,

00:04:46.893 --> 00:04:49.010
because they are not as
prone to those problems.

00:04:49.010 --> 00:04:51.685
When we get to the specifics
about survival and rescue,

00:04:51.685 --> 00:04:53.126
eh,

00:04:53.126 --> 00:04:54.265
2 out of 10.

00:04:54.265 --> 00:04:55.730
I know of one person from Iceland

00:04:55.730 --> 00:04:58.240
who survived conditions like
this, and that's a miracle.

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:00.860
Mac,

00:05:00.860 --> 00:05:03.458
two or three sips a day, yeah?

00:05:04.580 --> 00:05:06.272
We've got to make it last.

00:05:07.460 --> 00:05:09.795
If you're not in control
of your situation,

00:05:09.795 --> 00:05:12.659
you've got to try and find
something that you can control.

00:05:12.659 --> 00:05:16.109
And that's generating a
small routine of tasks.

00:05:21.230 --> 00:05:23.266
Heliographs are a brilliant survival item.

00:05:23.266 --> 00:05:25.511
Packed in most military survival kits.

00:05:25.511 --> 00:05:29.140
Each little reflective square,
each inch of reflectivity

00:05:29.140 --> 00:05:31.070
will project the sun back about 10 miles.

00:05:31.070 --> 00:05:33.294
So a 2-inch square like
that's good for 20 miles,

00:05:33.294 --> 00:05:35.029
which for the size and
weight of it's phenomenal.

00:05:35.029 --> 00:05:36.934
This is a pretty accurate view

00:05:36.934 --> 00:05:38.393
of what people look
like after they've baked

00:05:38.393 --> 00:05:40.313
in the sun in a life raft for a while.

00:05:40.313 --> 00:05:42.682
And what you can do to
try and prevent that

00:05:42.682 --> 00:05:43.920
is use anything for shade.

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:45.748
So, commonly, people will
take their shirts off

00:05:45.748 --> 00:05:47.395
and use it as a shade shelter.

00:05:47.395 --> 00:05:49.700
All life rafts these days have
got shade shelters on them.

00:05:49.700 --> 00:05:51.301
You're gonna get blistered and burned.

00:05:51.301 --> 00:05:52.762
What people have actually tried to do,

00:05:52.762 --> 00:05:54.790
if they've got no medicine
supplies with them,

00:05:54.790 --> 00:05:56.720
is any grease they can find.

00:05:56.720 --> 00:05:58.677
There was a guy in the
Second World War who,

00:05:58.677 --> 00:06:00.151
in his post-action report,

00:06:00.151 --> 00:06:02.294
described using his finger
and any little bit of grease,

00:06:02.294 --> 00:06:04.352
like maybe behind his
ear, trying to use that

00:06:04.352 --> 00:06:07.183
to parched lips that crack and bleed.

00:06:12.061 --> 00:06:14.540
[bird squawking]

00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:16.260
People will catch sea birds in life rafts

00:06:16.260 --> 00:06:17.170
and try to eat them.

00:06:17.170 --> 00:06:19.290
One of the best things
to pack as survival food

00:06:19.290 --> 00:06:21.722
is a can of dog or cat food,
because you'll only eat it

00:06:21.722 --> 00:06:24.080
if you're really hungry.
Same with an albatross.

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:26.505
What people try to do to
make it more palatable

00:06:26.505 --> 00:06:27.513
is normally to dry it.

00:06:27.513 --> 00:06:29.830
So if they've got any kind of thin lines,

00:06:29.830 --> 00:06:30.770
they can dry it in the sun,

00:06:30.770 --> 00:06:33.116
so you get a kind of seabird jerky.

00:06:33.116 --> 00:06:34.883
I don't think I can do it.

00:06:37.986 --> 00:06:39.394
We've got to try.

00:06:41.020 --> 00:06:42.035
[retching]

00:06:42.035 --> 00:06:44.570
Only eat it if you think
it won't make you sick.

00:06:44.570 --> 00:06:45.946
If you're ill and you're sick,

00:06:45.946 --> 00:06:47.799
you lose the calories you've
actually got in your stomach,

00:06:47.799 --> 00:06:50.180
and the fluid that's with
it, which is worse for you.

00:06:50.180 --> 00:06:52.547
But the problem with
foods like that, proteins,

00:06:52.547 --> 00:06:55.013
is it takes fresh water to digest.

00:06:55.013 --> 00:06:56.529
And the bigger problem that survivors

00:06:56.529 --> 00:06:58.646
have at sea is water acquisition,

00:06:58.646 --> 00:07:00.825
because you need water far
sooner than you need food.

00:07:00.825 --> 00:07:02.556
We can survive for weeks without eating,

00:07:02.556 --> 00:07:04.540
but you can't survive more
than a few hours or days

00:07:04.540 --> 00:07:06.705
in scorching sun without water.

00:07:06.705 --> 00:07:07.698
So if you eat protein,

00:07:07.698 --> 00:07:09.595
it'll exacerbate the not-drinking part,

00:07:09.595 --> 00:07:11.070
which is one of their major problems.

00:07:11.070 --> 00:07:12.561
If you haven't got loads of water,

00:07:12.561 --> 00:07:13.636
don't eat the albatross.

00:07:17.560 --> 00:07:19.260
It's long enough for you
to try and grab the tail

00:07:19.260 --> 00:07:20.390
away from the bitey end.

00:07:20.390 --> 00:07:22.670
What you can do, you know,
he's giving it a good shearing.

00:07:22.670 --> 00:07:23.800
What you can do with sharks,

00:07:23.800 --> 00:07:26.190
if you catch a small enough
one, is turn it upside down.

00:07:26.190 --> 00:07:27.193
When you flip them upside down,

00:07:27.193 --> 00:07:28.501
they go into a tonic state,

00:07:28.501 --> 00:07:30.570
which means they're sort
of, "Oh, what's happening?"

00:07:30.570 --> 00:07:31.960
I'd give this one 10 out of 10.

00:07:31.960 --> 00:07:34.960
This is the scenario that
we train our aircrew for,

00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:36.760
surviving in a life raft at sea

00:07:36.760 --> 00:07:38.012
where the life raft is something

00:07:38.012 --> 00:07:39.921
that's packed along with
the aircraft survival kit.

00:07:47.770 --> 00:07:50.610
They are far more at risk
from us than we are from them.

00:07:50.610 --> 00:07:52.460
But great whites specifically

00:07:52.460 --> 00:07:55.621
are very unlikely to
take mammals from rocks.

00:07:55.621 --> 00:07:56.750
You see orcas do it.

00:07:56.750 --> 00:07:57.900
Killer whales will do it.

00:07:57.900 --> 00:08:00.729
But, you know, great white
sharks, it's unlikely.

00:08:00.729 --> 00:08:02.650
What they're trying to
do is just get easy meat.

00:08:02.650 --> 00:08:04.783
If you're on a rock, you're not easy food.

00:08:06.940 --> 00:08:08.281
Can you outswim a shark?

00:08:08.281 --> 00:08:10.031
No. [laughs]

00:08:10.031 --> 00:08:11.470
Should you try? No.

00:08:11.470 --> 00:08:14.679
When sharks attack, there's
not a lot you can do about it.

00:08:14.679 --> 00:08:16.080
If you're in a group, if you're huddled,

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:17.647
try and stay together.

00:08:17.647 --> 00:08:19.417
Don't start smashing the water

00:08:19.417 --> 00:08:20.800
or doing any violent movements,

00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:22.092
'cause that'll attract them.

00:08:22.092 --> 00:08:23.786
You want to be unattractive to this thing.

00:08:23.786 --> 00:08:24.973
You want to look and feel

00:08:24.973 --> 00:08:26.823
like something it's not used to eating.

00:08:30.230 --> 00:08:31.890
It all depends on what kind of jellyfish,

00:08:31.890 --> 00:08:33.482
and goodness knows what
they're supposed to be.

00:08:33.482 --> 00:08:36.900
But some of them are lethally
dangerous, aren't they?

00:08:36.900 --> 00:08:37.966
You swim into jellyfish,

00:08:37.966 --> 00:08:39.823
the shark's definitely
gonna have a munch at you.

00:08:39.823 --> 00:08:41.350
Even if it's just an exploratory bite

00:08:41.350 --> 00:08:42.330
'cause you're bleeding,

00:08:42.330 --> 00:08:43.600
that could be enough to sever an artery,

00:08:43.600 --> 00:08:44.742
and then you'll bleed out.

00:08:48.250 --> 00:08:50.417
Sharkskin's incredibly thick.

00:08:50.417 --> 00:08:52.170
The thinnest part is on the nose.

00:08:52.170 --> 00:08:54.087
So if you did have the
choice, which you won't have,

00:08:54.087 --> 00:08:55.932
and you did have a weapon,
which you probably won't have,

00:08:55.932 --> 00:08:57.920
and you were gonna have a go, then, yeah,

00:08:57.920 --> 00:08:59.399
go for it around the nose.

00:08:59.399 --> 00:09:02.237
Entertainment rating, oh,
I don't know, 7 out of 10.

00:09:02.237 --> 00:09:04.026
Realism rating, maybe a 2.

00:09:05.170 --> 00:09:06.837
Where's the beach?

00:09:06.837 --> 00:09:07.720
Frank, wake up!

00:09:07.720 --> 00:09:09.020
It's not uncommon for people to drift

00:09:09.020 --> 00:09:11.260
that out of range of
getting back into land.

00:09:11.260 --> 00:09:12.522
It's a really common way for people

00:09:12.522 --> 00:09:13.663
to get into difficulties.

00:09:13.663 --> 00:09:15.520
One of the first senses you lose

00:09:15.520 --> 00:09:17.630
in a survival situation
is your sense of humor.

00:09:17.630 --> 00:09:18.810
And you know that in your mates.

00:09:18.810 --> 00:09:21.873
If they've ever had a
real crisis in their life

00:09:21.873 --> 00:09:23.030
and it's not right to joke about it,

00:09:23.030 --> 00:09:24.060
they won't joke about it.

00:09:24.060 --> 00:09:27.720
In a life raft, what
you can do is take five.

00:09:27.720 --> 00:09:29.870
Now, really difficult if you're two people

00:09:29.870 --> 00:09:31.503
in a one-person raft.

00:09:31.503 --> 00:09:33.152
But if it's any other sort of scenario,

00:09:33.152 --> 00:09:36.318
either shut up for five
minutes or just take a break.

00:09:36.318 --> 00:09:37.935
You know, just take that time out.

00:09:37.935 --> 00:09:39.823
Frank: It should've been you!
Mac: No!

00:09:39.823 --> 00:09:42.728
Luckily, life-raft knives
have got a blunted end,

00:09:42.728 --> 00:09:43.690
and they float,

00:09:43.690 --> 00:09:45.209
'cause that's the next
thing that would happen.

00:09:45.209 --> 00:09:47.500
Are you talking about
killing and eating me, Frank?

00:09:47.500 --> 00:09:51.258
Maybe I am, maybe I ain't, rum ham.

00:09:51.258 --> 00:09:53.158
Certainly, in the accounts
that I've come across

00:09:53.158 --> 00:09:56.000
from British mariners,
if you're in a life raft

00:09:56.000 --> 00:09:57.720
and it's all getting a bit difficult

00:09:57.720 --> 00:09:59.488
and everyone's really, really hungry,

00:09:59.488 --> 00:10:01.525
then one of you is gonna get eaten.

00:10:01.525 --> 00:10:04.194
They used to draw lots in
life rafts back in the day,

00:10:04.194 --> 00:10:07.211
like 1700s and so, even up until then.

00:10:07.211 --> 00:10:08.647
Normally, it was the young cabin boy

00:10:08.647 --> 00:10:09.526
that got knocked on the head,

00:10:09.526 --> 00:10:12.220
so all the old sweats have
probably rigged the draw.

00:10:12.220 --> 00:10:14.278
But, yeah, they'd draw lots.

00:10:14.278 --> 00:10:16.157
You don't need to eat that urgently.

00:10:16.157 --> 00:10:18.255
We can survive for weeks without food.

00:10:18.255 --> 00:10:22.210
So, yeah, cannibalism,
not that high on the list.

00:10:22.210 --> 00:10:25.002
All I'm saying is that when
you go into survival mode,

00:10:25.002 --> 00:10:26.420
it's every man for himself.

00:10:26.420 --> 00:10:28.690
What is survival mode? I don't know.

00:10:28.690 --> 00:10:30.524
I know it's not every man for himself.

00:10:30.524 --> 00:10:32.070
It's everyone together.

00:10:32.070 --> 00:10:33.829
Realism, 10 out of 10.

00:10:34.913 --> 00:10:36.670
That's how people that
haven't thought about it

00:10:36.670 --> 00:10:38.616
and haven't been trained
will probably react.

00:10:39.558 --> 00:10:40.966
No, no, no!

00:10:45.610 --> 00:10:46.934
So, by trying to free the mast

00:10:46.934 --> 00:10:48.566
that was bashing into
the side of the boat,

00:10:48.566 --> 00:10:50.450
she's unfortunately got
tangled up in the sail

00:10:50.450 --> 00:10:51.832
that's now dragging her down.

00:10:51.832 --> 00:10:53.395
One of the things we teach pilots to do

00:10:53.395 --> 00:10:54.545
is get away from their parachute,

00:10:54.545 --> 00:10:55.995
'cause it'll do exactly the same.

00:10:55.995 --> 00:10:57.443
But you can actually breathe through them.

00:10:57.443 --> 00:10:58.693
So, there's a little air pocket,

00:10:58.693 --> 00:10:59.976
so she did the right thing there.

00:11:03.750 --> 00:11:05.335
Looking good, mama.

00:11:05.335 --> 00:11:06.427
Duct tape's amazing, isn't it?

00:11:06.427 --> 00:11:07.537
You can fix most things with it.

00:11:07.537 --> 00:11:09.412
It's commonly put in survival packs.

00:11:09.412 --> 00:11:10.888
You've got to test somewhere first.

00:11:10.888 --> 00:11:12.652
You've got to make sure
it's clean and dry.

00:11:12.652 --> 00:11:14.060
Difficult at sea, but it's a calm day.

00:11:14.060 --> 00:11:15.969
'Cause if there's any moisture,
that'll affect the adhesive.

00:11:15.969 --> 00:11:17.600
So, the first thing to
do is protect yourself

00:11:17.600 --> 00:11:19.196
from the hazards, which she's done.

00:11:19.196 --> 00:11:21.240
The next thing she needs
to do is get rescued,

00:11:21.240 --> 00:11:23.611
be located, as we call
it in military survival.

00:11:23.611 --> 00:11:25.432
So she's going right the
way through it logically,

00:11:25.432 --> 00:11:26.880
and she's doing exactly the right things,

00:11:26.880 --> 00:11:28.038
try to get rescued.

00:11:28.038 --> 00:11:30.128
And then later, when she's
got some kind of message

00:11:30.128 --> 00:11:31.240
for help out there, then, yeah,

00:11:31.240 --> 00:11:33.115
I'll worry about water and food.

00:11:39.470 --> 00:11:41.360
What we'll tend to do
is throw a line to them,

00:11:41.360 --> 00:11:42.800
if they're compos mentis.

00:11:42.800 --> 00:11:44.091
But if they're unconscious or if they're

00:11:44.091 --> 00:11:45.926
further away than we can throw the line,

00:11:45.926 --> 00:11:47.335
then we'll tie it around someone's middle,

00:11:47.335 --> 00:11:48.501
just like she's done.

00:11:48.501 --> 00:11:49.872
So, what she's doing

00:11:49.872 --> 00:11:51.970
is absolutely the same as
we would teach people to do.

00:11:51.970 --> 00:11:53.350
She hasn't got a life preserver on

00:11:53.350 --> 00:11:55.358
is the one thing I'd
pick her up for on that.

00:11:55.358 --> 00:11:57.300
[groaning]

00:11:59.760 --> 00:12:02.280
Pulling someone out of the
water is really hard work.

00:12:02.280 --> 00:12:03.642
If they've got a buoyancy aid on,

00:12:03.642 --> 00:12:06.840
what you can do is sort of
almost semi-submerse the body,

00:12:06.840 --> 00:12:09.166
and then the buoyancy
will give you a kick up.

00:12:09.166 --> 00:12:10.330
But with a high-sided vessel

00:12:10.330 --> 00:12:11.860
or relatively high-sided vessel like that,

00:12:11.860 --> 00:12:13.063
it's really tricky.

00:12:13.063 --> 00:12:15.710
And the other problem with
getting people out of the water,

00:12:15.710 --> 00:12:17.880
not a lot of folk know
about hydrostatic squeeze

00:12:17.880 --> 00:12:19.165
and post-rescue collapse.

00:12:19.165 --> 00:12:20.880
Prioritizing her survival options,

00:12:20.880 --> 00:12:22.470
protection, location, water, and food,

00:12:22.470 --> 00:12:23.794
that gets a 10 out of 10.

00:12:28.440 --> 00:12:30.940
[yelling]

00:12:32.500 --> 00:12:35.482
So, what can you do in this
situation if you're these guys?

00:12:35.482 --> 00:12:37.147
If there's a massive wave like that

00:12:37.147 --> 00:12:38.890
coming straight at the boat,
there's not a lot they can do.

00:12:38.890 --> 00:12:40.972
The reason he's accelerating is like

00:12:40.972 --> 00:12:42.320
when you're trying to
paddle out through a big set

00:12:42.320 --> 00:12:44.100
when you're surfing, you
try and go a bit faster

00:12:44.100 --> 00:12:46.502
to get beyond it before it breaks.

00:12:46.502 --> 00:12:47.450
And that's about all they can do.

00:12:47.450 --> 00:12:49.452
But it's wrong place, wrong time.

00:12:49.452 --> 00:12:50.963
You can't turn a boat round fast enough

00:12:50.963 --> 00:12:52.138
if the wave's coming that quick.

00:12:52.138 --> 00:12:53.233
If you get caught sideways on it,

00:12:53.233 --> 00:12:55.670
it's going to tumble and destroy it.

00:12:55.670 --> 00:12:56.680
You could try and outrun it,

00:12:56.680 --> 00:12:58.528
but that boat isn't built for speed.

00:12:58.528 --> 00:13:00.740
What could you do?

00:13:00.740 --> 00:13:03.314
Emergency position-indicator beacons

00:13:03.314 --> 00:13:05.532
that will broadcast a
satellite where you are,

00:13:05.532 --> 00:13:06.990
have one of those on your person,

00:13:06.990 --> 00:13:09.333
have a survival suit,
have a life preserver.

00:13:09.333 --> 00:13:10.820
And if the boat becomes untenable

00:13:10.820 --> 00:13:12.730
because the sea's just too big,

00:13:12.730 --> 00:13:14.530
if you look at the top of the bridge house

00:13:14.530 --> 00:13:15.496
on the fishing boat,

00:13:15.496 --> 00:13:17.502
that white canister is a life raft.

00:13:21.160 --> 00:13:22.477
Even when the crib is going off,

00:13:22.477 --> 00:13:24.150
when it's really big,
it's nothing like that.

00:13:24.150 --> 00:13:25.366
They do happen, though.

00:13:25.366 --> 00:13:27.888
There's varying theories
as to what creates them.

00:13:27.888 --> 00:13:30.006
Mostly it's down around
the South Africa region.

00:13:30.006 --> 00:13:32.519
There's an Antarctic
current that comes up,

00:13:32.519 --> 00:13:33.593
meets the warmer water,

00:13:33.593 --> 00:13:36.430
and creates this kind of enormous pitching

00:13:36.430 --> 00:13:38.750
that cuts broken ships
in half, in the past.

00:13:38.750 --> 00:13:39.865
This is a 10 out of 10.

00:13:39.865 --> 00:13:42.128
There's a seafarers'
institute for a reason,

00:13:42.128 --> 00:13:43.880
to look after the families
of those who get lost at sea.

00:13:43.880 --> 00:13:45.679
It's a really, really harsh environment.

00:13:47.088 --> 00:13:49.755
[water pouring]

00:13:52.270 --> 00:13:53.920
It's a typical kind of scenario

00:13:53.920 --> 00:13:55.022
where people will get into trouble.

00:13:55.022 --> 00:13:56.721
There's something like 300 or 400

00:13:56.721 --> 00:13:59.088
of those shipping containers
go into the sea every year,

00:13:59.088 --> 00:14:00.410
and they just float
around near the surface.

00:14:00.410 --> 00:14:01.976
So you're not going to
know, especially at night.

00:14:07.800 --> 00:14:09.313
It makes total sense.

00:14:09.313 --> 00:14:10.929
When things get wet with salt water,

00:14:10.929 --> 00:14:12.902
as it evaporates, like it
would do in that climate,

00:14:12.902 --> 00:14:14.357
it forms little salt crystals.

00:14:14.357 --> 00:14:16.778
He's checking out his
communications equipment.

00:14:16.778 --> 00:14:18.701
And by washing it off with fresh water,

00:14:18.701 --> 00:14:19.825
that'll get rid of the salt crystals

00:14:19.825 --> 00:14:21.136
that could stop them working.

00:14:21.136 --> 00:14:22.610
He'll let it dry in the sun.

00:14:22.610 --> 00:14:23.882
He's not using loads of water.

00:14:23.882 --> 00:14:25.080
You can see he's being really careful

00:14:25.080 --> 00:14:26.588
about measuring it out.

00:14:26.588 --> 00:14:28.750
And that should make his
sat phone work again later.

00:14:28.750 --> 00:14:30.581
And we can tell it's a sat
phone from the antenna.

00:14:30.581 --> 00:14:33.244
If the repair works, albeit
a little bit Heath Robinson,

00:14:33.244 --> 00:14:34.283
he can phone for help.

00:14:39.860 --> 00:14:41.760
If you've been on a life
raft for even a few hours,

00:14:41.760 --> 00:14:43.035
it gets uncomfortable.

00:14:43.035 --> 00:14:44.228
If you've been in one for hours,

00:14:44.228 --> 00:14:46.886
days, weeks, months, terrible.

00:14:46.886 --> 00:14:48.393
And you get to your wit's end.

00:14:50.060 --> 00:14:51.223
So, in this scene,

00:14:51.223 --> 00:14:52.896
what Robert Redford's trying to do

00:14:52.896 --> 00:14:54.799
is just generate a little light.

00:14:54.799 --> 00:14:56.157
And it's difficult at night.

00:14:56.157 --> 00:14:57.611
I mean, the moon's bright behind him.

00:14:57.611 --> 00:15:00.023
And what you can do with a
mirror is reflect moonlight.

00:15:00.023 --> 00:15:02.160
But it's difficult on water
because of all the waves,

00:15:02.160 --> 00:15:03.650
the little ripples are reflecting.

00:15:03.650 --> 00:15:07.100
So what Robert's trying to
do is make a small fire,

00:15:07.100 --> 00:15:08.740
a contained fire in his raft.

00:15:08.740 --> 00:15:10.826
Now, that is fraught with danger.

00:15:10.826 --> 00:15:12.995
Typically, if people
run out of pyrotechnics,

00:15:12.995 --> 00:15:14.480
they're gonna scream and shout.

00:15:14.480 --> 00:15:16.817
But you can't be heard across open water.

00:15:16.817 --> 00:15:18.320
But what he could have done is,

00:15:18.320 --> 00:15:21.666
as well as packing some
pyrotechnical light devices,

00:15:21.666 --> 00:15:23.863
pack a laser flare,
because they're brilliant.

00:15:28.070 --> 00:15:30.730
This is not uncommon for
people to have to make

00:15:30.730 --> 00:15:34.403
these really critical
life-death decisions.

00:15:34.403 --> 00:15:36.700
And it's an all-or-nothing
thing, isn't it,

00:15:36.700 --> 00:15:37.540
if it doesn't work.

00:15:37.540 --> 00:15:39.500
There's an amazing anecdote
from Greenland in the war

00:15:39.500 --> 00:15:42.385
where three American airmen
who were stranded on the coast

00:15:42.385 --> 00:15:45.558
set fire to their warm
jackets in the winter,

00:15:45.558 --> 00:15:48.670
knowing that if people saw
the fire, they'd get rescued,

00:15:48.670 --> 00:15:50.600
and if they didn't,
they'd freeze to death.

00:15:50.600 --> 00:15:51.870
In terms of realism for the scene

00:15:51.870 --> 00:15:54.695
where Robert Redford makes a small fire,

00:15:54.695 --> 00:15:56.606
accidentally sets fire to his whole raft,

00:15:56.606 --> 00:15:57.941
realism, 9.

00:16:01.930 --> 00:16:05.430
Lots and lots of life rafts and lifeboats

00:16:05.430 --> 00:16:07.030
have got little manuals in them.

00:16:07.030 --> 00:16:09.660
And this is one of the
sort of standard diagrams

00:16:09.660 --> 00:16:12.040
that you'll see in it, like ocean currents

00:16:12.040 --> 00:16:14.423
and where the water will take you.

00:16:16.830 --> 00:16:18.178
I know it's all metaphor,

00:16:18.178 --> 00:16:20.914
but would you be able to
trail behind a metal lifeboat

00:16:20.914 --> 00:16:24.016
on a section of oars and life jackets?

00:16:24.016 --> 00:16:24.932
Eh.

00:16:24.932 --> 00:16:26.499
I mean, people have survived in worse.

00:16:26.499 --> 00:16:27.900
But that is pretty clever, isn't it?

00:16:27.900 --> 00:16:30.150
Triangles are one of the
strongest structures you can make.

00:16:30.150 --> 00:16:32.640
It's minimizing the amount
of knots he's got to tie.

00:16:32.640 --> 00:16:34.500
It's got a little bit of
give in it for the sea.

00:16:34.500 --> 00:16:37.245
Pi: "Establish a strict
schedule for eating,

00:16:37.245 --> 00:16:39.430
keeping watch, and getting rest."

00:16:39.430 --> 00:16:42.780
Spot on. Getting a routine
is absolutely critical.

00:16:42.780 --> 00:16:44.370
You can sense the passing of time

00:16:44.370 --> 00:16:45.870
by watching the movement of the sun.

00:16:45.870 --> 00:16:48.029
So if you put your arm out --

00:16:48.029 --> 00:16:49.155
and this is the same for all of us,

00:16:49.155 --> 00:16:50.087
'cause our hands and our arms

00:16:50.087 --> 00:16:51.281
are about the right proportion --

00:16:51.281 --> 00:16:53.843
that's 10 degrees, which
is just under an hour.

00:16:53.843 --> 00:16:56.080
15 degrees is how much
the sun moves in an hour.

00:16:56.080 --> 00:16:57.863
So you know that you've
got as many of those

00:16:57.863 --> 00:16:58.771
as it is for the time of year

00:16:58.771 --> 00:17:01.580
before sunrise and sunset.

00:17:01.580 --> 00:17:03.670
So there are things you can
do to keep track of time.

00:17:03.670 --> 00:17:06.230
Pi: "Do not drink urine or seawater."

00:17:06.230 --> 00:17:07.560
Yes, never. Stop!

00:17:07.560 --> 00:17:09.439
Right, everyone, are you hearing me?

00:17:09.439 --> 00:17:11.191
It will actually make you die quicker,

00:17:11.191 --> 00:17:12.962
because if you drink
anything that's salty,

00:17:12.962 --> 00:17:15.250
it exacerbates the problem of dehydration.

00:17:15.250 --> 00:17:17.730
Pi: "Above all, don't lose hope."

00:17:17.730 --> 00:17:19.763
The first point, I should say,
being a proper survival geek,

00:17:19.763 --> 00:17:21.491
is never cut into metal with your knife,

00:17:21.491 --> 00:17:22.740
'cause it'll just blunten it.

00:17:22.740 --> 00:17:24.010
But if you go to the Imperial War Museum,

00:17:24.010 --> 00:17:25.344
not far from where we're filming this,

00:17:25.344 --> 00:17:27.961
there's a boat in there from
the Anglo-Saxon we spoke about,

00:17:27.961 --> 00:17:29.283
and it's got notches in the gunnel

00:17:29.283 --> 00:17:30.983
for how long they survived at sea.

00:17:30.983 --> 00:17:33.580
And you will lose track if you
haven't got some form of log.

00:17:33.580 --> 00:17:35.874
And paper will get wrecked and ruined.

00:17:35.874 --> 00:17:38.815
So, yeah, just keep a mental thread

00:17:38.815 --> 00:17:39.948
of what's been happening.

00:17:39.948 --> 00:17:42.230
It's a good 8 or a 9, I think.

00:17:42.230 --> 00:17:43.728
Thanks for watching.
And if you enjoyed this,

00:17:43.728 --> 00:17:46.120
why not click on the video above?

